        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01F4LEmQ34siKMRuavyNP5S9",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/flag-debt.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Flag Debt\\ndescription: Accumulation of unused feature flags in code, creating testing and maintenance burden.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Flag-Debt\\n  - concept/Testing\\n  - concept/Technical-Debt\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source; concept well-established in feature-flag literature but not yet independently corroborated in wiki.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nFlag debt is the accumulation of **feature flags that have completed their rollout lifecycle but remain in the codebase** without removal. Each retained flag multiplies the number of code paths that must be tested, maintained, and debugged, even though the flag is no longer active or needed.\\n\\n## The Problem\\n\\nFeature flags are often used to decouple code deployment from release, allowing code to be shipped behind a flag and activated per-user, per-percentage, or per-environment without a redeploy. Once a rollout completes successfully and the flag is universally enabled (or the feature is rolled back and removed), the flag itself becomes candidate for cleanup.\\n\\nThe **operational risk** arises when cleanup is deferred or forgotten:\\n\\n- **Testing explosion**: Each flag represents a conditional branch. With N flags remaining, there are up to 2^N code paths that could theoretically execute. Test coverage grows combinatorially, and test maintenance becomes expensive.\\n- **Cognitive load**: Developers must reason about flag state when reading code, even for flags they did not write and may not understand.\\n- **Accidental flag resurrection**: Dead flags can be accidentally re-enabled, leading to unexpected behavior.\\n- **Configuration drift**: Over time, it becomes unclear which flags are active, which are dormant, and which should be considered obsolete.\\n\\n## Cleanup Discipline\\n\\nManaging flag debt requires explicit discipline:\\n\\n1. **Mark removal candidates early**: When a rollout completes, flag the flag for removal in the next cleanup cycle. Record the removal date in code comments or tracking systems.\\n2. **Establish removal SLOs**: Define a maximum flag lifespan (e.g., 2\\u20134 weeks post-rollout for transient flags, 90 days for feature flags under evaluation). Remove flags that exceed the SLO automatically or via scheduled review.\\n3. **Audit flag inventory regularly**: Maintain a registry of active flags. Run periodic scans to identify flags that are no longer referenced in configuration or that have no recent changes.\\n4. **Codify removal in CI/CD**: Make flag removal part of the deployment pipeline or review process, so flagged code does not accumulate unreviewed.\\n5. **Distinguish flag types**: Transient flags (for A/B testing or gradual rollout) have shorter lifespans than permanent toggles (for feature licensing or platform stability). Tailor cleanup schedules to flag category.\\n\\n## Related Concepts\\n\\n- [[concepts/autoscaling]] \\u2014 A different kind of operational tuning that must also be kept in check to avoid configuration debt.\\n- [[concepts/confidence-scoring]] \\u2014 Similar problem: metadata that is not actively managed can become stale and unreliable.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Good. The wiki is small and contains only two concept pages. The task directs me to focus on **flag debt and cleanup discipline**, and explicitly notes that the deploy/release decoupling angle is already covered elsewhere (not in the current wiki, but I should avoid duplicating it).\n\nThe source mentions two distinct topics:\n1. **Feature flags decouple deployment from release** (already covered elsewhere per instructions \u2014 don\u0027t duplicate)\n2. **Flag debt as an operational risk** (focus of the page)\n\nI\u0027ll create a new concept page focused on flag debt, its nature, and its cleanup discipline. I\u0027ll acknowledge the decoupling use case but keep the emphasis on the debt problem and governance.",
