      "assistant_text": "---\n\n## Summary\n\nI have successfully integrated the source into the wiki. Here\u0027s what was done:\n\n### Pages Created\n1. **\u0060concepts/flag-debt.md\u0060** \u2014 A new concept page focused entirely on flag debt as an operational risk and testing burden, following the task\u0027s explicit instruction to avoid duplicating the deploy/release decoupling angle (already covered elsewhere in the wiki).\n\n### Page Content \u0026 Decisions\nThe page structure:\n- **Opens with a clear definition** of flag debt as unused flags that persist after rollout completion, multiplying code paths under test.\n- **The Problem section** explains the operational risks: testing explosion (exponential growth of code paths), cognitive load for developers, accidental flag resurrection, and configuration drift.\n- **Cleanup Discipline section** provides five concrete practices for managing flag debt: marking removal candidates early, establishing removal SLOs, auditing flag inventories, codifying removal in CI/CD, and distinguishing flag types with different lifespans.\n- **Related Concepts** section links to existing wiki pages with similar issues (autoscaling drift, stale metadata in confidence-scoring).\n\nThe source statement about deployment/release decoupling was acknowledged briefly in \u0022The Problem\u0022 section to provide context, but not expanded into a major topic\u2014following the operator\u0027s directive to avoid duplication.\n\n### Index \u0026 Log Updates\n- Added the new page to \u0060index.md\u0060 under the Concepts section with a description and source count (\u00221 Quelle\u0022).\n- Created \u0060log.md\u0060 with the first ingest entry, documenting the creation, the source reference, and the task ID.\n\n### Confidence Scoring\nSet to \u0060medium\u0060 because the concept is well-established in feature-flag literature and the source is authoritative, but there is only a single source in the wiki so far. No independent corroboration yet.\n\nAll pages follow the OKF v0.1 conventions: frontmatter populated, wikilinks used for internal references, appropriate type and tag taxonomy, and markdown links for external citations (none in this case).",
